# Line endings are a CI correctness problem here, not a style preference.
#
# This project is developed on Windows, so every file in the working tree has
# CRLF endings. CI runs on Linux, and .github/workflows/ci.yml checks that two
# COMMITTED, GENERATED files — payload-types.ts and the admin import map — still
# match what their generators produce. Regenerate a CRLF file on Linux and every
# line of it differs, so that check would fail on every run for a reason that
# has nothing to do with the code.
#
# `text=auto` fixes it at the only layer that can: git stores text files with LF
# and converts back to the platform's native ending on checkout, so Windows
# still gets CRLF locally, Linux gets LF, and the generators agree with the
# repository on both. Content detection leaves real binaries (the covers, the
# fonts, the PDFs) untouched.
#
# Without this file the check depends on each contributor's core.autocrlf
# setting, which is exactly the kind of invisible per-machine difference the
# audit's CI section exists to remove.
* text=auto

# Written and read by tools that only ever emit LF, on any platform.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
